    body: `
<p>English needs two ideas here, "there is" and "have". Cebuano covers both with one small word, <b>naa</b>. Its opposite, <b>wala</b>, covers "there isn't" and "don't have".</p>
<h3>naa: there is</h3>
<p>Put <b>naa</b> first, join <b>-y</b> to it, then name the thing. The <b>-y</b> is a short <b>og</b>, the small word for "some" or "a". So <b>naay</b> means "there is some".</p>
{{ex:Naay tubig.|nah-ai TOO-beeg|There's water.}}
{{ex:Naay kape sa kusina.|nah-ai kah-peh sah koo-SEE-nah|There's coffee in the kitchen.}}
<h3>naa + ko: I have</h3>
<p>Cebuano has no verb for "have". It says "there is, mine, some": <b>Naa koy tubig.</b> The short pronoun <b>ko</b> goes in second place, right after <b>naa</b>, and the <b>-y</b> moves onto it. For "you have", use <b>ka</b>: <b>Naa kay…?</b></p>
{{ex:Naa koy tubig.|nah-ah koy TOO-beeg|I have water.}}
{{ex:Naa kay kuwarta?|nah-ah kai koo-wahr-tah|Do you have money?}}
<p>In a shop or a sari-sari store, you ask the staff as a group: <b>Naa ba moy…?</b> Here <b>mo</b> is short for <b>kamo</b> (you all).</p>
{{ex:Naa ba moy tubig?|nah-ah bah moy TOO-beeg|Do you have water? (in a shop)}}
<h3>naa: is here, is there</h3>
<p>Without the <b>-y</b>, <b>naa</b> says where someone or something is. Names still take <b>si</b>, and other nouns take <b>ang</b>.</p>
{{ex:Naa ba si Ana?|nah-ah bah see ah-nah|Is Ana there?}}
{{ex:Naa sa kusina ang tubig.|nah-ah sah koo-SEE-nah ahng TOO-beeg|The water is in the kitchen.}}
<h3>wala: there isn't, don't have</h3>
<p><b>wala</b> works the same way: <b>walay</b> for "there is no", <b>Wala koy…</b> for "I don't have", and <b>Wala si…</b> for "is not here".</p>
{{ex:Walay tubig.|wah-lai TOO-beeg|There's no water.}}
{{ex:Wala koy kuwarta.|wah-LAHQ koy koo-wahr-tah|I don't have money.}}
{{ex:Wala si Pedro.|wah-LAHQ see peh-droh|Pedro isn't here.}}
<h3>aduna: the careful word</h3>
<p><b>aduna</b> means the same as <b>naa</b> for "there is" and "have". You will meet it in writing and in careful speech: <b>Adunay tubig</b>, <b>Aduna koy…</b>. For where someone is, keep <b>naa</b>. In everyday talk, <b>naa</b> is the word you will use most.</p>
{{ex:Aduna koy pangutana.|ah-doo-nah koy pah-ngoo-tah-nah|I have a question.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using <b>dili</b> (no, not) for "there isn't". <b>Dili tubig.</b> → <b>Walay tubig.</b> For "there is no" and "don't have", the word is always <b>wala</b>.</div>
`,
